   "search_text": "2. There are n places set for tea around a circular table, and every place\n   has a small cake on a plate. Alice arrives ﬁrst, sits at the table, and\n   eats her cake (but it isn’t very nice). Next the Mad Hatter arrives,\n   and tells Alice that she will have a lonely tea party, and that she must\n   keep on changing her seat, and each time she must eat the cake in\n   front of her (if it has not yet been eaten).  In fact the Mad Hatter\n    is very bossy, and tells Alice that, for i = 1, 2, . . . , n −1, when she\n   moves for the i-th time, she must move ai places and he hands Alice\n   the list of instructions a1, a2, . . . , an−1. Alice does not like the cakes,\n   and she is free to choose, at every stage, whether to move clockwise or\n   anticlockwise. For which values of n can the Mad Hatter force Alice\n   to eat all the cakes?\n",
